post.description:
Creates a new section in the specified document. The resource_type attribute determines which type of section is created and which additional attributes are required.

Resource Types and their specific attributes:

- Document::Text: content
- Document::Heading: content, level (1-6)
- Document::Gallery: (no additional attributes)
- Document::Step: content, duration (optional), reset-count (optional)

IMPORTANT: The “rendered-content” attribute is READ-ONLY and automatically generated for Text and Step sections. Do not include it in your request - it will be ignored. When creating or updating sections, use only the “content” attribute with your HTML.

The request body must contain a JSON object with the resource-type attribute.

Example request (Text section):

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "resource-type": "Document::Text",
      "content": "<p>This is new text content.</p>"
    }
  }
}
```

Example request (Heading section):

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "resource-type": "Document::Heading",
      "content": "Chapter 1",
      "level": 1
    }
  }
}
```

Example request (Step section):

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "resource-type": "Document::Step",
      "content": "<p>Perform this step.</p>",
      "duration": 5,
      "reset-count": false
    }
  }
}
```

Example request (Gallery section):

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "resource-type": "Document::Gallery"
    }
  }
}
```

Note: Step sections automatically create an associated gallery for images.

patch.description:
Updates an existing section. Only attributes specific to the section's resource_type can be updated. The resource_type itself cannot be changed.

A PATCH request does not require all attributes - only those you want to update. Any attributes you don't specify will remain unchanged.

IMPORTANT: The “rendered-content” attribute is READ-ONLY and automatically generated. Do not attempt to include it in your update requests - it will be ignored. When updating content, use only the “content” attribute with your HTML, and the “rendered-content” will be automatically regenerated with processed inline image URLs.

The request body must contain a JSON object, for example:

Update Text section content:

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "content": "<p>Updated text content.</p>"
    }
  }
}
```

Update Heading level and content:

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "content": "Updated Heading",
      "level": 2
    }
  }
}
```

Update Step attributes:

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "content": "<p>Updated step instructions.</p>",
      "duration": 5,
      "reset-count": true
    }
  }
}
```

Update section sort order:

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "document-sections",
    "attributes": {
      "sort": 5
    }
  }
}
```

Updates an existing document. Returns the updated object if successful.

A PATCH request does not require that the entire object resource with all of its attributes be enclosed. Any attributes you don't specify will remain unchanged.

The request body must contain a JSON object, for example:

```
{
  "data": {
    "type": "documents",
    "attributes": {
      "name": "Document Name",
      "archived": true,
      "restricted": false,
      "public": false
    }
  }
}
```

